We can write a set by specifying the underlying set its elements are drawn from, then giving a condition the elements must meet in order to be included in the set.\textrm{e.g} \qquad X = \{ x \in \mathbb{N}\ |\ 1 \leq x < 6 \} = \{ 1,2,3,4,5\}

Here X is the set we are defining, and its elements are drawn from the natural numbers \mathbb{N}. In order to be included in this set, a natural number x must satisfy \leq x < 6. The number 6 is not strictly less than itself, so it is not included in the set.

The condition can involve other variables, as long as they are also well-defined.Y = \{ x \in \mathbb{N}\ |\ x = 2k, k \in \mathbb{N} \} = \{ 2, 4, 6, 8, \ldots\}

If the condition cannot easily be expressed using algebra, it can instead be stated in words.Z = \{ x \in \mathbb{N}\ |\ x \textrm{ has exactly two prime factors} \} = \{ 4, 6, 9, 10, 14, 15, \ldots\}

Note

When reading this notation aloud, the vertical line | separating the underlying set from the condition can be read as ‘such that’. A colon : is sometimes used in place of the vertical line.

Example 1.2

Write the following as an expression in set-builder notation.

(i) Let P be the set of all positive integers between 2 and 16 inclusive.

(ii) Let Q be the set of all positive integers that are divisible by 3.

(iii) Let R be the set of all positive even integers.

(iv) Let S be the set of all positive integers which are one more than a multiple of 5.

Solution

(i) P = \{ x \in \mathbb{N}\ |\ 2 \leq x \leq 16\} ( = \{ 2, 3, \ldots, 16\})

(ii) Q = \{ x \in \mathbb{N}\ |\ \frac{x}{3} \in \mathbb{N} \}

(iii) R = \{ x \in \mathbb{N}\ |\ x = 2k, k \in \mathbb{N}\}

(iv) S = \{ x \in \mathbb{N}\ |\ x = 5k+1, k \in \mathbb{N}\}

The natural numbers, \mathbb{N}

A natural number is a positive whole number.\mathbb{N} = \{ 1,2,3,4, \ldots \}

Note: some definitions of \mathbb{N} include zero; it can be useful to clarify which you mean. We use the term ‘positive integers’ to mean \{1,2,3, \ldots \} and ‘non-negative integers’ or sometimes ‘whole numbers’ to mean \{0,1,2,3, \ldots\} (which is also occasionally denoted \mathbb{N}_0).

The integers, \mathbb{Z}

The integers are the positive and negative whole numbers, including zero:\mathbb{Z} = \{ \ldots, -3, -2, -1, 0, 1, 2, 3, \ldots \}

We can also define the positive and negative integers:\mathbb{Z}^+ = \mathbb{N} = \{ 1, 2, 3, \ldots \} \qquad \mathbb{Z}^- =\{ -1, -2, -3, \ldots \}

The rational numbers, \mathbb{Q}

A rational number is a ratio of two integers in the form \frac{p}{q} where q \neq 0.\mathbb{Q} = \{ x\ |\ x = \frac{p}{q}, p,q \in \mathbb{Z}, q\neq 0\}

The irrational numbers, \mathbb{Q}'

An irrational number is one which cannot be written as a ratio of two integers \frac{p}{q}.\mathbb{Q}' = \mathbb{R}\setminus \mathbb{Q}= \{ x\ |\ x \neq \frac{p}{q}, p,q \in \mathbb{Z}\}

The real numbers, \mathbb{R}

Real numbers are the collection of all rational and irrational numbers.\mathbb{R} = \mathbb{Q} \cup \mathbb{Q}' = \{ x\ |\ -\infty < x < \infty \}

We can also define the positive and negative real numbers:\mathbb{R}^+ = \{ x \in \mathbb{R}\ |\ x > 0\} \qquad \mathbb{R}^- =\{  x \in \mathbb{R} | x < 0\}

The complex numbers, \mathbb{C}

Complex numbers are numbers of the form z =a + ib where a and b are real numbers and i is the imaginary unit \sqrt{-1}.\mathbb{R} = \{ z = a +ib\ |\ a,b \in \mathbb{R} \}

Note

These sets use a form of notation known as blackboard bold, or doublestruck, in which one or more of the vertical or diagonal lines of the letter are drawn twice to distinguish it from a regular letter.

When drawing these symbols by hand, you don’t need to include as much detail as the typeset versions have - as long as there’s a double stroke to distinguish it from a normal letter.

Many useful examples of sets can be defined as chunks of \mathbb{R} - usually, the set of numbers between a pair of given endpoints.

Definition 1.2 - Interval

An interval of the real line is a finite section of \mathbb{R} defined in terms of its endpoints. Intervals can be open or closed, depending on whether they include or exclude their endpoints.

Closed interval: [a,b] = \{ x \in \mathbb{R}\ |\ a \leq x \leq b \}

Open interval: (a,b) = \{ x \in \mathbb{R}\ |\ a < x < b \}

Half-open interval: [a,b) = \{ x \in \mathbb{R}\ |\ a \leq x < b \}
or (a,b] = \{ x \in \mathbb{R}\ |\ a < x \leq b \}

A square bracket at an endpoint indicates it is included in the set, whereas a parenthesis (round bracket) indicates it is
not included.

Example 1.3

Write the following intervals as a set of inequalities on x.

(i) [2,4]

(ii) (0.5,0.75]

(iii) (c,d)

Write the following inequalities as intervals of \mathbb{R}.

(iv) A = \{ x \in \mathbb{R}\ |\ 1 \leq x < 4 \}

(v) B = \{ x \in \mathbb{R}\ |\ 20 \geq x \geq 18 \}

(vi) C = \{ x \in \mathbb{R}\ |\ 0.2 < x < 0.3 \}

Solution

(i) [2,4] = \{ x \in \mathbb{R}\ |\ 2 \leq x \leq 4 \}

(ii) (0.5,0.75] = \{ x \in \mathbb{R}\ |\ 0.5 < x \leq 0.75 \}

(iii) (c,d) = \{ x \in \mathbb{R}\ |\ c < x < d \}

(iv) [1,4)

(v) [18,20]

(vi) (0.2,0.3)

Definition 1.8 - Set complement

The complement of a set A, denoted by A^C or A', is the set of all elements under discussion that are not in the set A. In set-builder notation, A^\mathsf{c} can be expressed asA^\mathsf{c} = \{x\ |\ x \in U, x \not\in A\}

For example,

Let U = \mathbb{R}, the set of all real numbers. If A is the set of negative real numbers including zero - i.e.A = \{ x\ |\ -\infty <x \leq 0\}

Then A^\mathsf{c} is the set of positive real numbers - i.e.A^\mathsf{c} = \{ x\ |\ 0 < x < \infty\}

Let U = \mathbb{Z}^+, the set of all positive integers. If A is the set of even
integers including zero, i.e.A=\{0, 2, 4, 6, \ldots\}

Then A^\mathsf{c} is the set of odd integers, i.e.A^\mathsf{c} = \{3, 5, 7, \cdots \}

Let U be the set of students at a university, and let C and N denote, respectively, the
commuting and non-commuting students in U. ThenC^\mathsf{c} = N \textrm{ and } N^\mathsf{c} = C

Definition 1.9 - Set difference

The difference of two sets A and B, also called the relative complement of B with respect to A, is denoted by A\setminus B, and is the set of elements which belong to A but which do not belong
to B; that is:A \setminus B = \{ x\ |\ x \in A, x \not\in B \}

The set A\setminus B can be read as “A minus B”. Some texts denote A \setminus B by A - B.

For example,

Consider the sets A=\{1, 2, 3,4\}, B=\{3, 4, 5, 6, 7\} and C=\{6, 7, 8, 9\}. ThenA\setminus B=\{1, 2\} \qquad B\setminus C =\{3, 4, 5 \}

B\setminus A =\{5, 6, 7\} \qquad C\setminus B =\{8, 9\}

Let \mathbb{N} be the set of natural numbers and A be the set of odd natural numbers. Then, the set
\mathbb{N} \setminus A is the set of even natural numbers; or\mathbb{N} \setminus A = \{x\ |\ x=2k, k\in \mathbb{N}\}

Example 1.5

Write down the following sets, in list form.

(i) \{ 6, 7, 8, 9, \ldots \}^\mathsf{c}, where U = \mathbb{N}

(ii) the consonants^\mathsf{c}, where U = the alphabet

(iii) \{ 1, 2, 3, \ldots, 10\} \setminus \{ 4, 5, \ldots 8\}

(iv) \{ \textrm{square, circle, triangle} \} \setminus \{ \textrm{circle, triangle}\} 

(v) (\mathbb{Z} \setminus \mathbb{N}) \setminus {0}

Solution

(i) In \mathbb{N}, \{ \}^\mathsf{c} = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5}$

(ii) In the alphabet, the consonants^\mathsf{c}= the vowels

(iii) \{ 1, 2, 3, \ldots, 10\} \setminus \{ 4, 5, \ldots 8\} = \{1, 2, 3, 9, 10\}

(iv) \{ \textrm{square, circle, triangle} \} \setminus \{ \textrm{circle, triangle}\} = \{ \textrm{square}\}

(v) (\mathbb{Z} \setminus \mathbb{N}) \setminus {0} = \mathbb{Z}^{-} (the negative integers)

We will see more examples of ways to combine and modify sets in the 

section on Set Operations.

Definition 1.10 - Union

The union of two sets A and B, denoted  A \cup B, is the set of elements that lie in A or B (or both); that is,A \cup B = \{x\ |\ x \in A \textrm{ or } x \in B\}





For example, let A = \{2, 4, 5\} and B = \{1,4,6,8\}. Then, A \cup B = \{1, 2, 4, 5, 6, 8\}. (Recall that each element in a set need only be listed once.)

So far, in discussing sets, the order of the elements of a set has been irrelevant. Here, we
introduce the concept of ordered sets, where the order in which the elements are listed is
important.

We use the notation (a,b) to indicate an ordered pair of objects a and b. This can be thought of as the set of two elements, \{a,b\}, together with the condition that a comes before b.

Definition 1.9 - Cartesian Product

Let A and B be any two sets. The Cartesian product of A and B, denoted A \times B, is the set of all ordered pairs such that the first member of the ordered pair is an element of A and the second member is an element of B.A \times B = \{(x, y)\ |\ x \in A \textrm{ and } y \in B\}

Similarly, the Cartesian product of three sets A, B and C can be expressed as:A \times B \times C = \{(x, y, z)|x \in A, y \in B \textrm{ and }z \in C\}

The Cartesian product of n sets A_1, A_2, A_3, \ldots , A_n is the set of all ordered n-tuples (a_1,a_2,a_3,\ldots ,a_n), where a_i \in A_i for i = 1,2,3, \ldots n .A_1 \times A_2 \times \ldots \times A_n = \{(a_1, a_2, a_3, \ldots , a_n)\ |\ a_i \in A_i, i = 1,2,3, \ldots n\}

For example,

If A = \{0,1,2\} and B = \{2,3\}, then the Cartesian product of A and B is given as:A \times B = \{(0,2), (0,3), (1,2), (1,3), (2,2), (2,3)\}

If A = \{1,2\} and B = \{p, q, r\}, then the Cartesian product of A and B is given as:A \times B = \{(1, p), (1, q), (1, r), (2, p), (2, q), (2, r)\}

If A = \{1,2\}, B = \{m, n\} and C = \{p, q\}, then the Cartesian product of A, B and C
is given as:A \times B \times C = \{(1, m, p), (1, m, q), (1, n, p), (1, n, q),

(2, m, p), (2, m, q), (2, n, p), (2, n, q)\}
